  "id": "Compound::DB00775 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "Tirofiban, an organonitrogen heterocyclic compound and antiplatelet agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Coronary Artery Disease through multiple interconnected mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Tirofiban, through its role in decreasing platelet aggregation, may contribute to the prevention of thrombotic events, which are a key driver of Coronary Artery Disease. This is supported by the inclusion of Tirofiban in the pathway leading to Decreased Platelet Aggregation, which in turn is linked to Dipyridamole and Clopidogrel, both of which are known to treat Coronary Artery Disease. Additionally, Tirofiban's association with Clopidogrel, which itself is used to treat Coronary Artery Disease, further reinforces the potential therapeutic relationship. The shared ancestry of Tirofiban and Clopidogrel within the Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compound class underscores a common pharmacological profile, suggesting that Tirofiban may exert its effects through a mechanism that is broadly applicable to the treatment of coronary vascular disease.",
